# Swift Code Patterns

Full conventions: `docs/conventions/CODING_CONVENTIONS.md`

## Project-Specific Patterns

### Settings — always through `Store.shared`, never `UserDefaults`

```swift
// GOOD
Store.shared.targetCode = code
let sc = Store.shared.shortcut(for: "translate")

// BAD — skips persistence and the onChange hook that re-registers hot-keys
UserDefaults.standard.set(code, forKey: "targetLanguageCode")
```

A new setting is a `@Published` property on `Store` whose `didSet` writes to `UserDefaults` and calls `onChange?()` when the menu or hot-keys depend on it. Decode persisted dictionaries by merging over the defaults, so an upgrade never drops a key.

### Main-actor by default

```swift
// GOOD
@MainActor
final class TranslatorPanel { /* ... */ }

// Cancel and supersede rather than queueing; the generation number stops a cancelled
// request writing state after a newer one started.
task?.cancel()
generation += 1
let mine = generation
task = Task { [weak self] in await self?.run(mine) }

// BAD — background queue touching UI state
DispatchQueue.global().async { self.statusItem.button?.image = image }
```

`HotKeyCenter` is the one non-isolated class: it is driven by a Carbon C callback and hops back with `DispatchQueue.main.async` before calling app code. SwiftUI views are `struct`s and static-only namespaces (`KeyDisplay`, `Languages`) need no isolation, so neither belongs in that count. Pure helpers, and helpers that must run off the main actor, may be `nonisolated static func` (see `LLMTranslator.stream`). Escaping closures that outlive the call capture `[weak self]`.

### Long-running model calls race a timeout

```swift
try await withThrowingTaskGroup(of: String.self) { group in
    group.addTask { /* real work */ }
    group.addTask { try await Task.sleep(nanoseconds: 20_000_000_000); throw TimeoutError() }
    defer { group.cancelAll() }
    return try await group.next()!
}
```

A stalled request must never wedge the app. Retry transient errors once; never retry a timeout or a cancellation. When the call streams, retry only while nothing has been emitted — retrying mid-stream duplicates words already on screen.

### Failure is feedback, not a crash

```swift
// GOOD — the popover is open, so it explains itself there
setPhase(.failed("The translation took too long and was stopped. Try again."))

// GOOD — user-actionable failure with no popover on screen
notify(error.localizedDescription)   // NSAlert with a specific, fixable explanation

// BAD
try! SMAppService.mainApp.register()
```

No `try!`, no `fatalError`. Use `try?` for best-effort side effects, `do`/`catch` + `NSAlert` when the user can fix it and nothing else can carry the message. Model framework enums get `@unknown default`.

### Privacy is a hard invariant

No `URLSession`, no sockets, no analytics, no `print`/`os_log` of user text, no third-party packages, and **no permission requested at all** — no `CGEvent`, no Accessibility check. Easy Write reads the pasteboard and never writes it, except in `Clipboard.write` behind the popover's Copy button; a test fails the build if a second write appears.

### Comments — explain WHY, not WHAT

```swift
// GOOD — decodes a magic number
.init(keyCode: 6, modifiers: 4608),   // ⇧⌃Z

// GOOD — records a platform gotcha
// Assigning a menu would swallow the click that has to reach the button's action.
statusItem.menu = nil

// BAD — narrates the next line
// set the busy icon
setIcon(busy: true)
```

Use `///` doc comments on types and non-obvious methods to state the contract or the reason the thing exists, never to restate its name. Use `// MARK:` to group sections in larger types.

### Formatting

4 spaces, never tabs. Braces on the same line. Wrap around 100–110 columns (a long user-facing string may run over rather than be split awkwardly). Underscore separators in long numeric literals: `20_000_000_000`, `120_000`.

### Types

`final class` for reference types, `struct` for values, `enum` for closed sets and for static-only namespaces (`KeyDisplay`, `Languages`, `Clipboard`). Singletons are `static let shared` with a `private init()`.

### Files and frameworks

One primary type per file, named after it; a small satellite (a SwiftUI view for its controller) may share the file. Drop new `.swift` files straight into `Sources/EasyWriteCore/` if they are pure logic, or `Sources/EasyWrite/` if they touch AppKit or the model — SPM globs both directories, so no manifest edit is needed. A type in `EasyWriteCore` that the app uses must be `public`. A new **system framework** does need a `linkerSettings` entry in `Package.swift`.
